{"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"e29c28cb","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.002223,"end_time":"2026-10-18T19:34:25.420807+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-18T19:34:25.418584+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# Lightweight IDS for resource-constrained Edge IoT devices\n","**Project overview.** \n","\n","This notebook develops and evaluates machine learning models that detect cyberattacks in IoT network traffic, using the network portion of the TON_IoT dataset (UNSW), which contains Zeek-style flow records from an IIoT testbed labeled as normal or as one of several attack types. The data is first explored and cleaned, after which baseline models (logistic regression, random forest and LightGBM) are trained for binary detection (attack vs. normal) and then for multiclass attack typing. Because the target setting is resource-constrained edge devices, a full feature set is compared against a reduced set of features that could realistically be computed on-device, and model size and inference speed are measured alongside accuracy. Throughout, the validity of high scores is tested by checking for leakage (identifier columns, duplicates, grouped data), and performance is reported using precision, recall, macro-F1 and PR-AUC rather than plain accuracy. The notebook closes with findings and limitations, including that the data originates from a simulated testbed and has not been validated on live traffic."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":1,"id":"fb77a4c7","metadata":{"_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19","_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-18T19:34:25.426473Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-18T19:34:25.425713Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-18T19:34:28.077997Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-18T19:34:28.076586Z"},"papermill":{"duration":2.658008,"end_time":"2026-10-18T19:34:28.080832+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-18T19:34:25.422824+00:00","s